-- blam.rofl — BLAM: what a diff hits, and WHY.
--
-- The domain is a monorepo build graph. Bazel, Nx and Turborepo compute the
-- affected set correctly and fast; none of them explains it. Two questions
-- are asked every day and have no answer in any of them:
--
--   * why did CI rebuild THIS, I never touched it
--   * why did CI NOT rebuild THIS, I just broke it
--
-- Both are derivation-tree questions, so both are free here: `why` walks the
-- support down to the changed file, `whynot` walks it down to the front where
-- the reachability walk stopped.
--
-- Layout of this file:
--   1. the packages, and what each depends on
--   2. the file -> target ownership map
--   3. the declared cost of each target (data; no rule reads it)
--   4. the rules — two build the target graph, three are the inquiry
--
-- The diff is NOT in this file. It is one relation, `changed/1`, asserted per
-- run, because a build graph outlives any particular diff. In the REPL:
--
--   npm run repl examples/blam/blam.rofl
--   changed("packages/utils/src/str.ts").
--   ? affected(T)
--   why affected(t(web, build))
--   whynot affected(t(docs, build))
--
-- examples/blam/demo.ts runs exactly that, plus the semiring folds, and
-- checks every number against an independent transitive closure.

-- =========================================================================
-- 1. the packages
-- =========================================================================
-- Eight packages. `config` is the global one: it depends on nothing, and
-- every other package reaches it — that is the source of "why did the
-- whole world rebuild", the commonest real pathology in a monorepo.
--
-- The shape, drawn with edges pointing at what a package needs:
--
--                 config
--                /   |   \
--          core -+   |    +- docs
--         /   \      |      /
--        |     utils-+-----+
--        |     /   \
--        |   ui     +-- api
--        |    \        /   \
--        +-----+-- web      cli
--
-- The DIAMOND is utils -> {api, ui} -> web: two branches converging on web,
-- so a change in utils reaches web by two independent routes and the
-- counting semiring answers 2 rather than 1. `docs` is the target
-- unreachable from a utils diff — the subject for `whynot`.

package(config).
package(core).
package(utils).
package(api).
package(ui).
package(web).
package(cli).
package(docs).

pkg_dep(core,  config).
pkg_dep(utils, config).
pkg_dep(utils, core).
pkg_dep(api,   utils).
pkg_dep(api,   core).
pkg_dep(ui,    utils).
pkg_dep(web,   api).
pkg_dep(web,   ui).
pkg_dep(cli,   api).
pkg_dep(docs,  config).
pkg_dep(docs,  core).

-- =========================================================================
-- 2. ownership: which target is responsible for which file
-- =========================================================================
-- Ownership is many-to-many on purpose. `shared/schema/events.json` is owned
-- by TWO targets — api and ui both code-generate from it — which is the
-- ordinary case a package-per-directory model cannot express, and which is
-- why the affected set is computed from targets rather than directories.
--
-- Note that a test target can own files nobody depends on: nothing depends
-- on a test target, so a change to `packages/utils/test/str.spec.ts` rebuilds
-- exactly one thing. That asymmetry is the whole reason build and test are
-- separate targets.

owns(t(config, build), "config/build.config.json").
owns(t(config, build), "config/tsconfig.base.json").
owns(t(core,   build), "packages/core/src/index.ts").
owns(t(utils,  build), "packages/utils/src/str.ts").
owns(t(utils,  build), "packages/utils/src/num.ts").
owns(t(utils,  test),  "packages/utils/test/str.spec.ts").
owns(t(api,    build), "packages/api/src/client.ts").
owns(t(ui,     build), "packages/ui/src/button.ts").
owns(t(web,    build), "packages/web/src/app.ts").
owns(t(cli,    build), "packages/cli/src/main.ts").
owns(t(docs,   build), "packages/docs/src/gen.ts").

-- the one file with two owners
owns(t(api,    build), "shared/schema/events.json").
owns(t(ui,     build), "shared/schema/events.json").

-- =========================================================================
-- 3. what each target costs to run, in minutes
-- =========================================================================
-- Declared data, from CI history. NO RULE READS THIS: v0 has no aggregation
-- (LIMITS.md), so the ranking that uses it is host-side arithmetic over an
-- engine-computed provenance fold, and demo.ts says so where it prints it.
-- It lives here rather than in demo.ts because it is a property of the
-- repository, not of the script that asks about it.

minutes(config,   1,   1).
minutes(core,    40,  55).
minutes(utils,   25,  30).
minutes(api,     60,  90).
minutes(ui,      70,  45).
minutes(web,    240, 310).
minutes(cli,     20,  25).
minutes(docs,    35,  10).

-- =========================================================================
-- 4. the rules
-- =========================================================================
-- Five, and the carrier appears in none of them. The same five clauses are
-- read by the Boolean fixpoint (is it affected), by the counting semiring
-- (how many independent routes), by the tropical semiring (how deep) and by
-- the provenance semiring (which edges carry it). Rules as data: what a rule
-- MEANS is not part of what a rule IS.
--
-- The edge relation is `needs`, not `dep`, and the name is not cosmetic:
-- boot.rofl concludes into `dep/2` for its own rule-dependency graph, so a
-- domain `dep/2` silently merges with it. Loaded together, the target-graph
-- edges and boot's stratification edges become one relation, boot's `reach`
-- closes over both, and `why` on a domain fact starts quoting rules about
-- `concludes`/`premise_pos`. Nothing errors; the answers just stop meaning
-- what they say. Domain relation names are part of the model — see
-- README.md, "Where the kernel fought us".

-- -- the target graph, derived from the package graph ---------------------

-- a package's test target is built after its own build target
needs(t(P, test),  t(P, build))  :- package(P).

-- a package's build target is built after the build targets it depends on
needs(t(P, build), t(Q, build))  :- pkg_dep(P, Q).

-- -- the inquiry -----------------------------------------------------------

-- base case: the target that owns a changed file is reached in zero edges
reaches(F, T)  :- owns(T, F).

-- one dependency edge. `needs(T, U)` is named in the body, so it appears as an
-- axiom in every `why` tree: the answer is the CHAIN OF EDGES, not the word
-- "affected".
--
-- ACYCLICITY, AND WHY COUNTING IS SAFE HERE WITHOUT A CLOSURE OPERATOR.
-- =====================================================================
-- A build graph is acyclic by construction — a cycle in `pkg_dep` is a
-- configuration error that Bazel, Nx and Turborepo all reject at load, and
-- so does any build that terminates. So the support hypergraph of `reaches`
-- has no cycle, the counting fold converges by plain Kleene iteration, and
-- the number it reports is the number of distinct dependency PATHS from the
-- changed file to the target. Finite, exact, meaningful.
--
-- DO NOT COPY THIS REASONING INTO A DOMAIN WITH CYCLES. The instance used
-- here (runtime/semirings.ts `countingSemiring`) is declared CLOSED and
-- carries a `star`; on cyclic data it multiplies every fact on a cycle by
-- star(one) and correctly answers INFINITE, because a fact on a cycle really
-- does have unboundedly many derivations. That is the engine being right,
-- and it is a useless metric. examples/nope/ hits exactly that: an
-- assume-role graph has cycles, and the count there is only a number because
-- the model carries the path inside the fact so the SUPPORT graph is acyclic
-- even though the role graph is not. Here we need no such trick, and the
-- reason is a property of build graphs, not of these rules.
--
-- demo.ts proves both halves: it reports `cyclic: 0` on this graph, then
-- adds one forbidden edge (utils <-> ui) and shows the same semiring
-- instance answering "infinitely many".
--
-- WHAT THE COUNT MEANS HERE (f_counting_reads_oppositely_by_domain demands
-- every example say this, because the same metric reads in opposite
-- directions across domains). In BLAM the count is COUPLING, and a high one
-- is a DEFECT. Two routes from a diff to a target mean the target is dragged
-- into the rebuild by two independent chains, so removing either one changes
-- nothing and the rebuild is that much harder to stop; five routes mean the
-- target is wired into everything. That is the opposite sign to NOPE and
-- OOPS, where independent supports mean a conclusion SURVIVES losing one, and
-- opposite again to AKA, where many routes mean ambiguity. Read operationally
-- the number is the same fact as the cut analysis: the count is how many
-- routes a cut would have to break, and a count of 1 is the only case a
-- single edge can fix. The metric is one metric; its sign belongs to the
-- domain, and here the domain says lower is better.
reaches(F, T)  :- needs(T, U), reaches(F, U).

-- the diff. One relation, asserted per run. A target is affected once for
-- each (changed file, route) pair that arrives at it, and that multiplicity
-- is what the counting semiring folds.
affected(T)    :- changed(F), reaches(F, T).
